math-academy-id "q-73636" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $B,$ given by\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $B$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $B$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342046 :question/math-academy-id "q-73639" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 0 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 0 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $4$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $2$nd, $3$rd, $5$th and $6$th columns."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367373 :question/math-academy-id "q-131307" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -6 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -6 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $4$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $2$nd, $3$rd, $5$th, and $6$th columns."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400590 :question/math-academy-id "q-131302" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{4} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402448 :question/math-academy-id "q-35731" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $2$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402461 :question/math-academy-id "q-35757" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403682 :question/math-academy-id "q-73638" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{4} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $4$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th columns."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573261 :question/math-academy-id "q-73637" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns."}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}] :topic/title "Identifying the Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}
